This folder contains an application that, instead of performing
calculations in the evaluation function, waits for a predefined
amount of time. As this makes the run time independent of the underlying
calculation and e.g. the load on client- and server-systems, this
allows us to measure the theoretically possible speedup for a given
amount of data exchange and length of the evaluation procedure, dependant
on the local environment. Furthermore, we can measure the overhead that
is jointly introduced by the consumer/submission infrastructure, serialization
and network transfers.

The speed-up is taken against a serial reference, which is a run of its own:

  ./GParallelisationOverhead --reference

It measures the delays of config/GDelayIndividual-reference.json on one local
worker, whatever consumer is named, fits a line through the run times and
stores it in referenceLine.txt (--referenceFile names another file). It also
checks that the evaluations did run one after another. The reference needs
only a few short delays; the line is extrapolated to the longer ones.

The measurement itself is a second run with the consumer of your choice, for
example a server and its clients:

  ./GParallelisationOverhead -c beast
  ./GParallelisationOverhead -c beast --client      (once per worker)

It reads the stored reference, runs the delays of config/GDelayIndividual.json
and writes the plot. The reference and the measurement must use the same
config/GEvolutionaryAlgorithm.json. Both halves used to run in one process;
an algorithm submits through the one consumer of its process, so that
reference ran on the same workers as the measurement.

***
ATTENTION: This test can run for a VERY long time
***

The result is a ROOT script (fullResults.C unless the configuration names
another file) that shows the speed-up over the evaluation time twice, with
a linear and with a logarithmic speed-up axis. You can look at it with a command similar to

root -l fullResults.C

The numbers behind the plot -- the fitted serial run time, the measured
parallel run time and the speed-up, each with its error -- are written to
shortDelayResults.txt (short_result_file in the configuration).

provided you have installed ROOT on your system (see http://root.cern.ch
for further information).
